## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design practical backpressure and degradation strategies for a CNPA stack under load, balancing user impact with data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and load shedding
- Degraded paths and asynchronous processing
- Idempotency and deduplication across HTTP, DB, and Kafka
- Observability: latency, error rate, queue depth, saturation signals

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst; // max tokens
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  consume(n = 1) {
    this._refresh();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
  _refresh() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement end-to-end idempotency to prevent duplicates during backpressure? 
- What metrics and rollback criteria would you rely on to trigger a full rollback or canary pause?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to fuse transactional boundaries with event delivery and downstream idempotency, a common production risk area.

## Key Concepts

- Transactional outbox pattern across DB and message bus
- Idempotent Kafka producers and deduplication keys
- Downstream lake ingestion deduplication using ingest_id
- Failure/retry handling and exactly-once semantics

## Code Example

```sql
CREATE TABLE cnpa_outbox (
  id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_id UUID NOT NULL,
  event_type TEXT NOT NULL,
  payload JSONB NOT NULL,
  occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
);
```

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE cnpa_user SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO cnpa_outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload, occurred_at)
VALUES ($3, 'UserUpdated', to_jsonb($4), NOW());
COMMIT;
```

